<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Savona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Savona is a port city on the Ligurian coast of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. A rival of Genoa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was subdued by the Genoese in 1528. The della Rovere family of Popes Sixtus IV and Julius II came from the area, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> captive in the city from 1809 to 1812.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/savona.jpg" alt="Savona" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roberta Salamone · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Savona_(260099167).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Savona is a seaport and municipality in the west of the Italian region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> and the capital of the Province of Savona. It faces the Ligurian Sea, between the sea and the Ligurian Alps, 40 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, and is the main city of the western section of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-riviera">Italian Riviera</a>. The navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> is associated with the town. A cottage in the Savona hills is called Columbus's house, and a house in the neighbourhood is documented as the property of his father Domenico Colombo, where the family lived for many years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ligurian tribes lived here in ancient times, and the city, which had been allied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>, came under Roman influence about 180 BCE. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> it passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> rule in 641, being destroyed in the attack. It recovered as a county seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>. In the tenth century its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were counts of Savona, and the countship passed to the Marquesses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> in 981 and to the Marquesses Del Vasto in 1084.</p><p>After a long struggle against the Saracens, Savona became independent in the eleventh century as a free commune allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and its freedom declaration was signed in 1191 in the Torre del Brandale. Two monasteries, one Dominican and one Franciscan, made it a centre of religious culture from the thirteenth to the sixteenth century. It then fought the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and was definitively conquered in 1528. The Genoese destroyed the upper town and buried the port, and in 1542 they built the Priamar fortress on the site of the old cathedral and old city. Savona shared the fortunes of Genoa until Napoleonic times.</p><p>Austrian forces besieged the city between April and mid-May 1800 while a small British naval force blockaded it, and the fortress surrendered on 15 May. From 1809 the city held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a>, a prisoner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, for a few years. Savona was annexed to the Kingdom of Sardinia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> in 1815 and later became part of the unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. In 1830 and 1831 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a> was imprisoned in the Priamar. In the twentieth century Savona became a regional industrial hub.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Cappella Sistina beside the Cathedral of the Assumption was built from 1480 to 1483 and holds the mausoleum that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-rovere">Della Rovere</a> family erected for his parents. Cardinal Giulio della Rovere, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>, built the Palazzo Della Rovere, designed by Giuliano da Sangallo as a university. The Torre Leon Pancaldo, built in the fourteenth century, is the symbol of the town. The Priamar now holds the Museum Centre of Priamar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Savona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…said he never heard &quot;Bella ciao&quot; sung in twenty months of partisan war, and historians of Italian music including Antonio Virgilio <b>Savona</b> and Michele Straniero agree that it was sung rarely if at all; Cesare Bermani and Ruggero Giacomini hold that some brigades sang some version of it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bella-ciao">Bella ciao</a></li></ul></section>
